bitcoin critics for a long time have  been talking about the fact that they  believe the u.s government and other  governments around the world are going  to ban ownership of bitcoin now of  course there is precedent for government  stepping in and banning ownership of  assets go back to the 1933 there was an  executive order that the president  signed and eventually there was multiple  pieces of legislation put forward that  forced americans to stop hoarding gold  the idea was that the federal reserve  needed access to more gold and they  couldn't shut down gold production and  so instead they actually needed to ramp  up their access and so what they said  was you as a private citizen are not  allowed to hoard gold you must turn it  in there was a fixed price right around  20 at the time they said you have to  give us your gold we'll pay you for it  and then you are not allowed to hoard it  moving forward and so these bitcoin  critics they've been saying the same  thing that the government the central  bank and militaries around the world  they may not be able to shut down the  bitcoin network but actually what we  should anticipate is that those  governments are going to move in and  they're going to ban ownership of  bitcoin now of course this seems kind of  outrageous in today's day and age that a  government would move in to actually ban  ownership of an asset especially when  politicians hold the asset our largest  financial institutions hold the asset  and other nation states around the world  are starting to adopt it as legal tender  well the bitcoin critics didn't see what  happened yesterday coming  yesterday a sitting u.s congressman  representative tom emmer put forward a  proposal not to ban bitcoin ownership  but actually to ban the federal reserve  from issuing directly to american  citizens a central bank digital currency  that's right the u.s sitting congressman  did not ban bitcoin instead he is trying  to ban a central bank digital currency  he said as other countries like china  develop cbdc's that fundamentally omit  the benefits and protections of cash it  is more important than ever to ensure  the united states is digital currency  policy protects financial privacy  maintains the dollar's dominance and  cultivates innovation he then said  cbdc's that fail to adhere to these  three basic principles could enable an  entity like the federal reserve to  mobilize itself into a retail bank  collect personally identifiable  information on users and track their  transactions indefinitely not only does  this cbdc model raise single point of  failure issues leaving americans  financial information vulnerable to  attack but it could be used as a  surveillance tool that americans should  never be forced to tolerate from their  own government requiring users to open  an account at the fed to access the  united states cbdc would put the fed as  an insidious path akin to china's  digital authoritarianism  any cbdc implemented by the fed must be  open permissionless and private this  means that any digital dollar must be  accessible to all transact on a  blockchain that is transparent to all  and maintain the privacy elements of  cash in order to maintain the dollar  status as the world's reserve currency  in a digital age it is important that  the united states leads with a posture  that prioritizes innovation and does not  aim to compete with the private sector  simply put we must prioritize blockchain  technology with american characteristics  rather than mimic china  digital authoritarianism out of fear  this is absolutely fascinating this is a  sitting u.s politician who is saying the  united states  we have to preserve financial privacy we  have to encourage innovation and we have  to continue to be the shining light on a  global stage for economic prosperity a  central bank digital currency if used  incorrectly would actually violate those  three principles it could actually hurt  the us dollars global reserve status it  could infringe on americans financial  privacy and it could actually decelerate  our pace of innovation china is serving  as the example of exactly what  authoritarian governments want to do  they want to use it for financial  surveillance they want to prevent their  citizens from doing certain things  that's not what the principles that  america was built on america shouldn't  implement a central bank digital  currency if that's what we're going to  do  the federal reserve is already losing  trust  and people are questioning what their  motives are but if we were simply to  allow for a central bank digital  currency to be created by the central  bank and issued directly to americans  that would give the central bank and the  government the ability to know how much  money is in your bank account who are  you transacting with what are you  purchasing when how much that's  information that the government frankly  doesn't need it's an infringement on our  financial privacy  instead what we need is we need the  ability for americans to protect their  financial privacy we need the ability  for private enterprise to actually drive  innovation i don't want to trust the  central bank or my government to be  innovative i don't want them to be  competing on a global stage with other  companies in other countries when it  comes to technology the same people who  run the dmv  probably also who run our public health  policy who have proven themselves to be  incompetent in many ways why are we  going to trust them with a piece of  technology that quite literally our  country's reserve status  and also our place of economic  prosperity and leadership depends on  instead we're much better off having  private enterprise compete to produce  the single best asset and of course  bitcoin being a decentralized  transparent digital  open source project  has garnered over a trillion dollars of  value there's a reason why the free  market is determined bitcoin  specifically to have value  but also we have seen over a hundred  billion dollars of value flow into  stable coins those stable coins have the  same exact monetary policy as us dollars  but they're actually being created and  governed and distributed by private  companies the government isn't even  needed to actually get over 100 billion  dollars of value into these stable coins  and so if we're able to get the same end  result the same end benefit to users  around the world by using the private  enterprise rather than public  taxpayer money  or  organizations like the central bank  we probably should let the private  enterprises do what they do best compete  on the global stage because ultimately  the free market is going to be the  referee and we need to move quickly we  need to move efficiently and we need to  take this stuff seriously and so kudos  to representative emmer on his  courageous step to say listen  we should actually not ban bitcoin  ownership we should ban a central bank  digital currency being created and  distributed directly to americans by the  federal reserve
 

